{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01ET8ztMaPmTYBWFTUvFmDD9", "type": "tool_result", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"excerpt_removed\": true, \"references\": [{\"url\": \"https://news.mynavi.jp/article/20240109-2859430/\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.samac.jp/search/poems_detail.php?id=50\"}, {\"url\": \"https://s.awa.fm/track/a850fa0217c4c3076b49\"}, {\"url\": \"https://100poets.com/tag/waka-poetry/page/9/\"}, {\"url\": \"https://100poets.com/2012/07/20/the-morning-after-poem-50/\"}, {\"url\": \"https://collections.mfa.org/objects/234404\"}, {\"url\": \"https://100poets.com/category/japan/page/11\"}, {\"url\": \"https://online.bunka.go.jp/index.php/heritages/detail/186452\"}, {\"url\": \"https://online.bunka.go.jp/heritages/detail/186452\"}]}"}]}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-20T23:43:37.471Z", "tool_use_result": {"query": "君がため惜しからざりし命さへ 後拾遺和歌集 恋二 669 藤原義孝", "results": [{"tool_use_id": "srvtoolu_01Atdi74aWr5TqdwX7n3Sn46", "content": [{"title": "「君がため惜しからざりし命さへ 長くもがなと思ひけるかな」は、小倉百人一首の50番目として選出されている有名な恋の和歌。作者は美男として知られていた藤原義孝です。", "url": "https://news.mynavi.jp/article/20240109-2859430/"}, {"title": "【嵯峨嵐山文華館】小倉百人一首の全首を見る - Poets Search -", "url": "https://www.samac.jp/search/poems_detail.php?id=50"}, {"title": "s.awa.fm", "url": "https://s.awa.fm/track/a850fa0217c4c3076b49"}, {"title": "The Morning After: Poem Number 50", "url": "https://100poets.com/tag/waka-poetry/page/9/"}, {"title": "The Morning After: Poem Number 50", "url": "https://100poets.com/2012/07/20/the-morning-after-poem-50/"}, {"title": "Advanced Search", "url": "https://collections.mfa.org/objects/234404"}, {"title": "A Small Token: Poem Number 15", "url": "https://100poets.com/category/japan/page/11"}, {"title": "online.bunka.go.jp", "url": "https://online.bunka.go.jp/index.php/heritages/detail/186452"}, {"title": "online.bunka.go.jp", "url": "https://online.bunka.go.jp/heritages/detail/186452"}]}, "The search confirms the basic facts, but not the anthology volume or poem number you gave.\n\n- **Attribution:** The poem is the 50th in the *Ogura Hyakunin Isshu* and is credited to Fujiwara no Yoshitaka (954–974), a son of Kentoku-kō and father of Fujiwara no Yukinari. He died of smallpox at the age of 21. He was one of the Thirty-Six Medieval Immortal Poets.\n- **Source anthology:** One listing gives the source as the *Goshūi Wakashū* (\"後拾遺集\") and the theme as a wish for love to last even beyond the life one would gladly give up. The topic is the wish for love's continuance: a man who would have given up his life for a lover now wants to live longer. The *Goshūi Wakashū* was compiled in 1086 by Fujiwara no Michitoshi at Emperor Shirakawa's command, according to the Agency for Cultural Affairs database.\n- **Context:** Scholars and sites describe it as a *kinuginu no uta*, a poem sent the morning after a night together. The term for this kind of poem is kinuginu no uta (後朝の歌), and were often a way of sharing feelings after the two have parted company.\n- **Not verified:** None of the results confirms that this poem is number 669 in the 恋二 (Love 2) section of the *Goshūi Wakashū*. Check that numbering against a full text of the anthology (for example, the National Institute of Japanese Literature's database)."], "durationSeconds": 7.847226792000001, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01ET8ztMaPmTYBWFTUvFmDD9", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
